.TH "CHECK_PORTS" "1" "April 2026" "NMS_Tools" "Deterministic Multi-Port TCP Checker"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
\f[B]check_ports\f[R] \[em] deterministic multi-port TCP availability
checker with service resolution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]check_ports\f[R] -H [-p ] [-s ] options
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]check_ports\f[R] is a deterministic, audit-transparent TCP port
checker used for monitoring, diagnostics, and automation.
It evaluates one or more TCP ports on a target host and produces
consistent, machine-parseable output suitable for Nagios, automation
pipelines, and operator workflows.
.PP
The tool supports: - Explicit ports (\f[V]-p\f[R]) and service names
(\f[V]-s\f[R]) - Multiple ports, ranges, and mixed lists - Deterministic
timeout behavior - JSON, verbose, quiet, and Nagios output modes -
Operator-grade logging with rotation and structured START/RESULT/END
banners - Strict evaluation rules for open / closed / timeout /
unreachable states
.PP
Service names are resolved using the system\[cq]s service database
(e.g.\ \f[V]/etc/services\f[R]).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Required Arguments
.PP
\f[B]-H, \[en]host \f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Target hostname or IP address.
.SS Port and Service Selection
.PP
\f[B]-p, \[en]ports \f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Comma-separated list of ports or ranges.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Examples: \f[V]80\f[R], \f[V]80,443\f[R], \f[V]8000-8100\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]-s, \[en]service \f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Comma-separated list of service names (e.g., \f[V]ssh\f[R],
\f[V]https\f[R], \f[V]mysql\f[R]).
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Each service is resolved to its associated TCP port.
.PP
At least one of \f[V]--ports\f[R] or \f[V]--service\f[R] must be
provided.
.SS Optional Arguments
.PP
\f[B]\[en]timeout \f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Per-port timeout.
Default: 5 seconds.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]require-all\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Return CRITICAL unless \f[I]all\f[R] ports are open.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]require-any\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Return OK if \f[I]any\f[R] port is open.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]fail-only\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Suppress output for successful ports in verbose or JSON modes.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]json\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Emit structured JSON output.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]verbose\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Emit human-readable diagnostics including resolved services, explicit
ports, and per-port results.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]quiet\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Suppress human-readable output (exit code only).
.PP
\f[B]\[en]log-dir \f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Directory for log files.
Logging is disabled if omitted.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]log-max-mb \f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Maximum log file size before rotation.
.PP
\f[B]\[en]version\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Show version and exit.
.SH EXIT CODES
.PP
\f[B]0\f[R] \[em] All required ports are open
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]1\f[R] \[em] One or more ports failed (WARNING)
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]2\f[R] \[em] Required ports failed (CRITICAL)
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]3\f[R] \[em] Unknown error (UNKNOWN)
.SH PORT STATES
.PP
\f[B]open\f[R] \[em] TCP connection succeeded
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]closed\f[R] \[em] Connection refused
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]timeout\f[R] \[em] No response before timeout
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]unreachable\f[R] \[em] Host unreachable or DNS failure
.SH OUTPUT MODES
.SS Nagios (default)
.PP
Single-line status suitable for monitoring systems.
.SS Single-service checks
.PP
OK - ssh (22) is open WARNING - mysql (3306) is closed
.SS Single explicit port
.PP
OK - Port 2222 is open WARNING - Port 8080 is closed
.SS Multi-port checks
.PP
WARNING - Closed ports: 22, 123 CRITICAL - Problem ports: 22, 123, 5000
.SS Verbose
.PP
Human-readable diagnostics including: - Host - Services requested -
Resolved service ports - Explicit ports - Combined port list - Per-port
results
.PP
Example: Host: db01 Services requested: mysql Service ports: 3306
Explicit ports: None All ports: 3306
.PP
db01:3306 = open
.SS JSON
.PP
Deterministic machine-parseable structure including: - explicit ports -
service ports - combined port list - per-port results - categorized
open/closed/timeout/unreachable lists
.SS Logging
.PP
If \f[V]--log-dir\f[R] is provided, the tool writes operator-grade logs
with rotation:
.PP
[START] cmd=\[lq]\&...\[rq] host=\&...
ports_explicit=[\&...]
ports_service=[\&...]
ports_all=[\&...]
[PORT] host=\&...
port=\&...
status=\&...
[RESULT] state=\&...
message=\[lq]\&...\[rq] [END]
.PP
Logs are written only in non-Nagios modes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Check a single service: \f[V]check_ports.py -H db01 -s mysql\f[R]
.PP
Check a single explicit port: \f[V]check_ports.py -H router -p 2222\f[R]
.PP
Check multiple services: \f[V]check_ports.py -H web01 -s https,ssh\f[R]
.PP
Check mixed explicit + service:
\f[V]check_ports.py -H app01 -s redis -p 8080\f[R]
.PP
Verbose diagnostics:
\f[V]check_ports.py -H cache01 -s redis --verbose\f[R]
.PP
JSON output: \f[V]check_ports.py -H db01 -s mysql --json\f[R]
.SH FILES
.PP
Logging is disabled unless \f[V]--log-dir\f[R] is explicitly provided.
.PP
When enabled, logs are written to the directory specified by
\f[V]--log-dir\f[R].
No default log directory is assumed or created.
